The `index.html` entrypoint must be a complete HTML document. Put the root
composition inside `<body>` and follow this contract:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"></head>
<body>
  <div id="root" data-composition-id="root" data-start="0" data-width="{{ width }}" data-height="{{ height }}">
    <!-- timed clips -->
    <script src="./vendor/gsap.min.js"></script>
    <script>
      const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
      // Add deterministic animations with explicit absolute positions.
      tl.set({}, {}, {{ duration_seconds }});
      window.__timelines = window.__timelines || {};
      window.__timelines["root"] = tl;
    </script>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```
The timeline must finish at exactly {{ duration_seconds }} seconds. Do not add `data-duration` or `data-fps` to the composition root.

Mounting a sub-composition. A file under `compositions/` is mounted from its host
by `data-composition-src`, and both sides carry metadata:
```html
<div id="beat-2" class="clip" data-composition-id="beat-2"
     data-composition-src="compositions/beat-2.html"
     data-start="4" data-duration="6" data-track-index="1"></div>
```
Each file you author under `compositions/` must wrap its content in `<template>`
and put `data-composition-id`, `data-width`, `data-height`, and `data-start` on a
root element inside it. A bare `<div>` with no `<template>` renders nothing.

Managed Hyperframe catalog: {{ catalog }}
Sfumato installs those items into the project for you, so never author, rename, or
inline their files, and never return them in your JSON. Mount an item only when the
plan selected it in that scene's `production.catalog_items`; treat every other ID
above as absent. A block is already a complete sub-composition that registers its
own paused timeline, so mount it exactly as above with
`data-composition-src="compositions/<id>.html"` and an empty host: never drive its
internals from your timeline, and never give the host a shorter `data-duration`
than the runtime shown in parentheses above. Blocks are authored at 1920x1080, so
on a different canvas scale the host with `gsap.set(...)` rather than editing the
block. Selecting a catalog item never satisfies the requirement that you author at
least one `compositions/*.html` scene of your own.
